---
term: Immanent authority
aka:
  - Human authority
section: authority
order: 20
definition: The principle that authority originates in human agency, not in an external or transcendent guarantor.
seeAlso:
  - derivative-authority
  - natural-resistance
  - thesis-of-immanent-authority
---
People don't need permission from a divine authority, a universal moral tribunal, an objective ethical order, or any other conception of *l'Autre* to set purposes, formulate principles, or act on their convictions. The ability to set one's own purposes and pursue them belongs to the condition of being an agent.

Immanent authority doesn't mean that everyone affected sees every exercise of power as legitimate. It means that the origin of authority doesn't lie outside humanity. So no individual, institution, government, or collective has an automatic claim to universal obedience. People who reject an authority keep their agency, their capacity to resist it, and their capacity to set up alternative arrangements.

The absence of an external moral guarantor doesn't mean that people can't establish ethical principles or judge actions by them. Moral judgments arise from the principles, values, and purposes through which people interpret and organize their existence. Such judgments can be internally coherent, sincerely held, and absolute within their foundations without binding everyone. People are at once the authors of their purposes, the agents of their realization, and participants in the consequences that follow.

Immanent authority doesn't establish that every asserted purpose is objectively true, or that every action is universally justified. It establishes that external moral authorization isn't a precondition for action.

Kant also places the source of the moral law in the agent. His *autonomy*, as opposed to *heteronomy*, is the will giving the law to itself (*Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals*, 1785). Metaethical constructivism, such as Korsgaard's *The Sources of Normativity* (1996), grounds morality in agency in a similar way. Both hold that the resulting morality binds every rational agent. Immanent authority makes no such claim of universality.
